#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fdf766-dca6-5dcc-ada6-c24a50c95716" :knowledge-point/title "Using Radioactive Dating to Estimate the Age of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef74b3a6-ea54-5296-b5de-f662f60f6903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.125$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367605} {:answer/id #uuid "18de7856-6904-5cfa-874d-cee4bb529a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.25$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367606} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6487d9-4ace-5001-a38b-eabb716a3bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12.5$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367607} {:answer/id #uuid "04df3361-f2dd-5d0e-b922-98fe3040f169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$50$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367608} {:answer/id #uuid "1806677e-9da0-5bca-84b4-900ac3902ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c6487d9-4ace-5001-a38b-eabb716a3bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12.5$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367607} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e3ecb4-a435-50af-bcae-f491b642011c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "112894ef-795f-5e88-92b6-8cecf3a4c053" :question/math-academy-id "q-119647" :question/problem "At a geological site, a sample of a lunar rock is found with $84%$ of its original rubidium-87 content. Given that the half-life of rubidium-87 is $49.72$ billion years, approximately how old is the sample?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=49.72$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n49.72=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.84{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.84{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.84{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.84\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.84 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{49.72\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈12.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $12.5$ billion years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2a81fbb-f1a8-5d80-942e-7a91bdea1408" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Radioactive Decay Given the Half-Life of a Substance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2d84dfd-c9e6-5003-ac7c-e9ea0ec3fd1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168\\,\\mu \\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367612} {:answer/id #uuid "01e53487-1b14-5372-8e4f-20071bc6c649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "257\\,\\mu \\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367613} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0b9056-1f22-5cc1-945f-a81d63b7230e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "185\\,\\mu \\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367614} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9a9e04-70d2-51fc-abd2-ad17a26de2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "154\\,\\mu \\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367615} {:answer/id #uuid "57904937-370c-5d16-b386-208372b8cdd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "178\\,\\mu \\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57904937-370c-5d16-b386-208372b8cdd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "178\\,\\mu \\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367616} :answer-field/id #uuid "32273387-4de6-5518-9523-f7f689666c55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eba20180-55df-5c61-831d-b8f257c1e722" :question/math-academy-id "q-120002" :question/problem "Samarium-147 has a half-life of $106.1$ billion years. A sample of samarium-147 has an initial mass of $300\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the mass of the sample after $80$ billion years?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=106.1$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n106.1=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{106.1}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=300\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=300{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/106.1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(80)=300{e}^{-80\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/106.1}≈178\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that there will be $178\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ of the sample left."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2a81fbb-f1a8-5d80-942e-7a91bdea1408" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Radioactive Decay Given the Half-Life of a Substance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "677520ea-2209-5c86-9d83-7003a2404f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367619} {:answer/id #uuid "662c228f-45b6-59b0-b6ae-d0b8458abef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "677520ea-2209-5c86-9d83-7003a2404f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367619} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f57081c-0f21-526d-ab87-a528d56fa49e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df0d388b-d8e7-5b69-9a20-a4a8e6fb4a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-353520" :question/problem "The half-life of cobalt-60 is $5.27$ years. How long does it take for a $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of cobalt-60 to reduce to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{yr}$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5.27$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5.27=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{8.0} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{16}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡16}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $4$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=4\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=4\\cdot 5.27=21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2a81fbb-f1a8-5d80-942e-7a91bdea1408" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Radioactive Decay Given the Half-Life of a Substance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67457bd7-88fc-5451-9373-e093db91fd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "548.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67457bd7-88fc-5451-9373-e093db91fd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "548.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367623} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9c6f2b4-fb25-536b-9947-ace029ba1cc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0f317b5-d8f7-537b-9329-3365ba5a0f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-353523" :question/problem "The half-life of fluorine-18 is $109.7$ minutes. How long does it take for a $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ sample of fluorine-18 to reduce to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{min}$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=109.7$ minutes, we have\n\n$$\n109.7=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=6.4\\,\\text{ng},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{0.2}{6.4} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{32} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{32}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡32}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =548.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.2\\,\\text{ng}}{6.4\\,\\text{ng}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $5$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=5\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=5\\cdot 109.7=548.5\\,\\text{min}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fdf766-dca6-5dcc-ada6-c24a50c95716" :knowledge-point/title "Using Radioactive Dating to Estimate the Age of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cbeaaa0-f83b-5e1f-81b5-53530e4d01ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cbeaaa0-f83b-5e1f-81b5-53530e4d01ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367626} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b693eb-17a4-55c0-9906-f969af7c9551" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "607cd4b5-ba88-56bb-b14d-3e00fe0f1f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-355651" :question/problem "At a deep-sea sediment site, a sample is found with $30%$ of its original beryllium-10 content. Given that the half-life of beryllium-10 is $1.39$ million years, how old is the sample? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe sample is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nmillion years old." :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=1.39$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n1.39=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1.39}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.30{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.30{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.30{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ 0.30\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ 0.30 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1.39} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1.39\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $2.4$ million years old."}]}
